#. type: Title #
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Partial differential equations {#partial-differential-equations}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This active area of research explores how quantum algorithms can help solve "
"partial differential equations that describe physical systems and processes,"
" such as fluid dynamics, heat transfer, electromagnetic fields, and quantum "
"mechanical systems. Approaches include quantum algorithms for linear systems"
" of equations, which can be applied to certain discretized differential "
"equations, as well as quantum methods for differential equations that "
"directly exploit quantum circuits or quantum state representations. These "
"techniques can offer computational advantages for specific large-scale "
"problems, particularly when the solution can be efficiently encoded in and "
"extracted from a quantum state. However, their practical benefits depend on "
"factors such as problem structure, data encoding, hardware requirements, and"
" the cost of retrieving the solution.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"A common workflow encodes a trial solution as a parameterized quantum "
"circuit, then classically optimizes the circuit parameters so that the "
"resulting quantum state approximates the true solution of the differential "
"equation across a set of sample points. This variational approach can "
"accommodate boundary and initial conditions, and can be combined with "
"multiple classical optimizers in sequence to improve convergence. The "
"application function on this page automates this workflow for specific "
"domains, so that you can supply physical parameters and boundary conditions "
"directly, without implementing the underlying encoding and optimization loop"
" yourself.\n"
msgstr ""

